## Verdict

SOLVED: no

The strongest defensible result is a narrow value-theoretic characterization for public, exogenous, horizon-aware minimax classes. It is exact, but it is essentially the minimax value in disguise, not the desired structural tail characterization or matching-rate theorem.

## Candidate Solution Or Main Attempt

For a horizon-indexed public-exogenous class \(\Theta_T\), finite grid \(G\), and randomized common policies, define

\[
V_\theta^G=\sup_{a\in G}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb E_\theta[a{\bf 1}\{M_t>a\}],
\qquad
M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,
\]

and let

\[
C_T^G=\{(L_\theta(A))_{\theta\in\Theta_T}: A \text{ admissible randomized }G\text{-policy}\}.
\]

Then the exact finite-grid arbitrary-class signed value is

\[
\mathcal R_T^G
=
\sup_{\nu\in ba_1^+(\Theta_T)}
\left\{
\langle V^G,\nu\rangle-\sup_{c\in C_T^G}\langle c,\nu\rangle
\right\}.
\]

With \(G_T=\{0,1/T,\ldots,(T-1)/T\}\),

\[
|\mathcal R_T^{[0,1]}-\mathcal R_T^{G_T}|\le 1.
\]

So, in this narrow public-exogenous minimax model,

\[
(\mathcal R_T^{[0,1]})_+=o(T)
\]

is equivalent up to \(O(1)\) to the positive part of the displayed `ba` dual being \(o(T)\).

This is a valid exact condition, but not a satisfying tail characterization. For finite \(\Theta_T\), the `ba` dual reduces to the ordinary least-favorable-prior Bayes gap

\[
\sup_{\pi\in\Delta(\Theta_T)}
\left[
\sum_\theta \pi_\theta V_\theta^{G_T}
-
\sum_t
\mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi}
\max_{a\in G_T}
a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t})
\right].
\]

## Concrete Lemmas Or Reductions

1. Known finite-horizon exogenous laws are oracle-learnable:
\[
R_T\le T/K
\]
by conditional grid maximization. Hence no fixed-law raw-tail necessity can hold under law-aware horizon-aware quantifiers.

2. Uniform truncation upper bound:
\[
\sup_{P\in\mathcal C_T}R_T^P
\lesssim
\sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}+T/K+\sup_{P\in\mathcal C_T}\delta_T^P(H).
\]

If \(\Delta_T(H)\le T H^{-\alpha}\), this gives

\[
R_T\lesssim
T^{(\alpha+1)/(2\alpha+1)}
(\log T)^{\alpha/(2\alpha+1)}
\]

with \(K=T\).

3. Finite-class exact Bayes identity holds on finite grids. Infinite classes require either the abstract `ba` dual or a no-gap/product-closure condition.

4. Hidden-terminal lower-bound atom:
for horizon \(T=D+1\), two hidden worlds give minimax regret at least

\[
qD/8,
\]

while having \(\beta(h)=q\) for \(h<D\) under absorbing extension and \(\Delta,\delta\asymp q(D-H)\).

## Gaps And Failure Points

The main unresolved piece is a structural necessary-and-sufficient tail condition. Per-law \(\beta,\Delta,\delta\) profiles cannot work.

Matching lower rates for power-tail envelopes remain open. The one-block \(qD\) atom gives only \(\Omega(D B(D))\), too weak for the truncation upper exponent.

Adaptive truncation is also open. It reduces to a comparator-specific heterogeneous-delay experts theorem, but the key two-arm delayed-advantage safety/capture bound is unproved.

The original Borel-policy no-gap bridge is credible under the exact finite-grid public-exogenous seed/state model, but still needs final measurable-version/randomization citations if promoted beyond the completed \(L^\infty\) kernel model.

## Counterexamples Or Obstructions

- iid uniform prices: \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but known-law regret can be zero.
- Tiny-price blocks: raw \(\beta(h)=1\), yet comparator value is uniformly bounded.
- Rare-history construction: raw essential-sup \(\beta(h)=1\), but \(\sup_TV_T<\infty\) and \(\Delta_T(H)=O(1/H)\).
- Hidden/labeled pair: identical per-world \(\beta,\Delta,\delta\) and reward profiles, but hidden class has \(\Omega(qD)\) regret while labeled class has zero regret.
- Infinite-class product-closure gap: finite subclasses can be easy while the full Borel-policy class has linear value.

## Promising Ideas To Explore

1. Write the accepted theorem package cleanly: finite-grid Bayes identity, grid-continuum comparison, `ba` dual, no-gap domination theorem, and \(\delta_T(H)\) upper bound.

2. Attack matching lower rates through limited-leakage or noisy common-parameter constructions, computing the finite-grid Bayes value first.

3. Work on the two-arm delayed-advantage problem:
\[
A_T\ge -\tilde O(\sqrt T),
\qquad
A_T\ge S_T-\tilde O(\sqrt{TD}).
\]

4. Develop last-exceedance/value-tail calculus, but keep observability and predictability in the condition.

## Notes For Critics

Do not read the `ba` dual as an ordinary Bayes/posterior formula. The support term is abstract for finitely additive priors.

Do not accept this as a scalar tail characterization. The hidden/labeled no-go blocks that.

All finite-horizon formulas must use horizon-censored \(M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s\).

The upper bounds assume public exogenous prices. Action-dependent markets need a separate causal model.
